ing in the gaps in the numbering scheme and covering file operations, permissions, user management, and system utilities.\n\nNow I'm looking at the remaining commands—vdir through vidir—and noting which requirement states are missing entirely, particularly for testutils, sys, format, cat, and the boolean utilities. The code status breakdown shows that the magefiles and sys implementations are done, but format and testutils still need work despite having complete specifications.\n\nI'm realizing there's a tension here: the constraint about only proposing tasks for items with \"ready\" status doesn't apply to these packages since they lack requirement_states entries altogether. But the package_contracts section already defines contracts for testutils, sys, format, and the utility packages, which means I need to check whether the constraint about not proposing implementation tasks without a contract task actually blocks these or not.\n\nLooking at rel01.2 with sponge, I see that R6.1 and R6.2 are marked \"ready\" in requirement_states even though the overall sponge use case shows as \"implemented\" — so these specific requirements haven't been tackled yet despite the broader implementation status.\n\nFor rel02.0, the trivial utilities like yes, basename, and dirname all have ready requirements waiting. And then there's rel01.3 with du and rel02.1 with tee, both having ready items as well.\n\nSo my priority list is: sponge's R6.1 and R6.2 first since they're in the earliest release, followed by du, then the rel02.0 utilities.\n\nBut I'm noticing format and testutils from rel00.0 aren't showing up in requirement_states at all, even though code_status marks them as not started. I think the safest approach is to stick with the constraint—only propose tasks for items that actually appear in requirement_states with ready status, since those are the ones being actively tracked.\n\nNow for the weights: the constraint says I need to check requirements.yaml, but I can't use tool