re—basic disk usage reporting and recursion probably don't depend on format at all. directory traversal, summary totals, output format, and exit codes. The basic output doesn't need special formatting—just size and path separated by a tab. Human-readable output is probably a later requirement, so I could implement the core functionality without the format package.\n\nI'm still uncertain though, and the stitch agent might hit blockers. Let me focus on what I can definitely unblock. The roadmap mentions that du \"requires pkg/sys and pkg/format for extended file metadata and human-readable size formatting\"—the word \"extended\" suggests format is only needed for advanced features, not the core. Since sys is already implemented, I should be able to move forward.\n\nBut I'm constrained to four tasks total. Sponge takes two, and yes takes three across twelve requirements, which fills all my slots. If I go with du instead, I'd have sponge in one task and du's first twelve requirements spread across three tasks.\n\nLooking at strict release priority: sponge has two ready requirements for rel01.2, du has nineteen ready requirements for rel01.3 (though format might block some), and yes, basename, and dirname have twelve, fifteen, and thirteen ready requirements respectively for rel02.0. If du isn't blocked on format, I could allocate tasks as sponge R6.1-R6.2, then du R1.1-R1.4, du R1.5 through R2.3, and du R2.4-R2.7. If du is blocked, I'd need a different approach.\n\nThe safer bet is du since it's the earlier release. The stitch agent can read the SRD and determine whether format is actually needed—core block counting shouldn't require it. If the agent does hit a format dependency, it can flag that and I can adjust.\n\nWait, but thinking about this from the agent's perspective: it reads the SRD, reads existing code, implements requirements, and runs tests. If du doesn't need format, great. If it does, the task fails. That violates the independence constraint—each task mus